::kind(api)

::meta{crate: tomet-type, dependencies: list(tomet-ast), dependents: list(tomet-expr, tomet-field-utils, tomet-semantics, tomet-style, tomet-validator-core, tomet-vault)}

=[tomet-type]

==[tomet-type]

The types a value can be declared as, and how data and text are held to
them.

==[Architecture & Responsibilities]

- ("One vocabulary") A `type:` is written in the settings' `meta` fields, in `::vars`, and in
a vocabulary's arguments. They all name a [`Type`] here, so a name that
one place accepts is a name every place accepts.
- ("Knows nothing about elements") This crate depends on [`tomet_ast::Value`] and nothing else. A type does
not know what an element is, which vocabulary declared it, or what
`::meta` is; the dependency is what keeps it so.
- ("What is held to a type") [`Type::admits`] asks whether a [`Value`] in a document is of the type.
[`Type::read_text`] turns a text that arrives as text -- `--var key=value` --
into the value of the type. [`Type::holds_text`] asks whether a string
is in the form the type gives text, for the types that have one.

==[What a type is not]

- It is not how a field is written or made. `format`, `offset`, `length`,

`prefix` and `always_newline` are properties of a field's declaration, and
belong to the formatter. In particular what makes a `nanoid` well formed
depends on the field's `prefix` and `length`, so [`Type::Nanoid`] admits a
string and judges no more; `tomet-format-field-utils` judges the rest.

- It is not a value in the evaluator. A datetime read here stays text, and

[`datetime`] gives its parts; `tomet-expr` turns it into its own value.

==[Not yet]

- A version with a pre-release or build part (`1.0.0-beta`, `+5`).
- A type with a parameter. `list` has no element type yet; the author expects

it to be written `<list<string>>` when it comes, so [`Type::parse`] reads
a name and nothing else, and says so for any other spelling.

- `enum`. Nothing gives it a meaning yet (`:rule`'s `allow:` takes the same

spelling and means a list of names).

==[Module datetime]

What text reads as a date, or a date and a time.

The one definition of what a `datetime` field holds. The evaluator reads a
field with it, `tomet check` checks a field with it, and `tomet format`
decides with it what to rewrite as RFC 3339. It gives the parts and
nothing else -- no clock type -- so a crate that only needs to know
whether text reads as a datetime does not carry one.

===[Structs]

====[struct DateTimeParts]

A date, or a date and a time, as written.

===[Functions]

====[fn parse_datetime_parts]

Reads a date, or a date and a time: `2026-10-17`, `2026-10-17T09:00`,
`2026-10-17 09:00:00`, `2026-10-17T09:00:00.5+09:00`.

- The date is `YYYY-MM-DD`; with nothing after it the time is midnight.
- The time follows after a `T` or a space, as `HH:MM`, or `HH:MM:SS`

with an optional fraction.

- An offset (`Z`, `+09:00`) may close it. Without one the value is read

as UTC, which is what a bare date has always been.

`None` for anything else, including a day the month does not have.

====[fn parse_offset_seconds]

Reads a UTC offset written `+09:00`, `-05:30` or `Z`, as seconds east of
UTC. `None` for anything else.

==[Structs]

===[struct Mismatch]

A value that is not of the type it was held to.

==[Enums]

===[enum Type]

A type a value can be declared as.

===[enum TypeError]

A `type:` that does not name a type.

===[enum TextError]

Why a text is not a value of a type.
